<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Convergence as a hypothesis: Parsons problems only, for now.          -->
<!-- Each proof applies a convergence hypothesis at a tolerance it chooses. -->
<section xml:id="sec-convergence_hypothesis" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Convergence as a Hypothesis</title>

  <p>
    Recall that <m>x_n\to L</m> if <m>\forall \, \epsilon\gt 0 \, \exists \, N\in\R</m> s.t. <m>\forall \, n\gt N, |x_n-L|\lt\epsilon</m>.
    In each problem below, a convergence statement is something we <em>know</em>, and the proof applies it with a tolerance of its own choosing.
  </p>

  <exercises xml:id="parsons-convergence-hypothesis">
    <title>Proof practice: using convergence</title>

    <!-- #5: tolerance sqrt(epsilon) -->
    <exercise label="parsons-squares-null" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide">
      <title>Squaring a sequence that converges to 0</title>
      <statement><div>Suppose <m>x_n\to 0</m>. Put the given blocks in a correct order to prove that <m>x_n^2\to 0</m>. Some blocks are not needed.</div></statement>
      <blocks>
        <block name="1" order="4">
          <div>We assume that <m>x_n\to 0</m>. We will prove that <m>x_n^2\to 0</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="2" depends="1" order="7">
          <div>Let <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="3" depends="2" order="2">
          <div>Because <m>x_n\to 0</m> and <m>\sqrt{\epsilon}\gt 0</m>, by definition there exists <m>N\in\R</m> such that for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>|x_n-0|\lt\sqrt{\epsilon}</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="4" depends="3" order="9">
          <div>We show that this <m>N</m> works for <m>x_n^2\to 0</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="5" depends="4" order="5">
          <div>Let <m>n\in\N</m> with <m>n\gt N</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="6" depends="5" order="10">
          <div>Since <m>n\gt N</m>, we have <m>0\leq |x_n|\lt\sqrt{\epsilon}</m>. Squaring nonnegative numbers preserves the inequality, so <m>|x_n^2-0|=|x_n|^2\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="7" depends="6" order="1">
          <div>Thus for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>|x_n^2-0|\lt\epsilon</m>. By definition, <m>x_n^2\to 0</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="8" correct="no" order="6">
          <div>Because <m>x_n\to 0</m> and <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>, by definition there exists <m>N\in\R</m> such that for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>|x_n-0|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="9" correct="no" order="3">
          <div>Choose <m>N=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{\epsilon}}</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="10" correct="no" order="8">
          <div>Because <m>x_n\to 0</m> and <m>\epsilon^2\gt 0</m>, by definition there exists <m>N\in\R</m> such that for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>|x_n-0|\lt\epsilon^2</m>.</div>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <!-- #7: Ross 8.6(a), tolerance epsilon in both directions -->
    <exercise label="parsons-abs-null" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide">
      <title>A sequence and its absolute values</title>
      <statement><div>Put the given blocks in a correct order to prove that <m>x_n\to 0</m> if and only if <m>|x_n|\to 0</m>. Some blocks are not needed.</div></statement>
      <blocks>
        <block name="1" order="5">
          <div>First, suppose <m>x_n\to 0</m>. We will prove that <m>|x_n|\to 0</m>. Let <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="2" depends="1" order="8">
          <div>Because <m>x_n\to 0</m> and <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>, by definition there exists <m>N\in\R</m> such that for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>|x_n-0|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="3" depends="2" order="2">
          <div>Let <m>n\in\N</m> with <m>n\gt N</m>. Then <m>\bigl||x_n|-0\bigr|=|x_n|=|x_n-0|\lt\epsilon</m>. By definition, <m>|x_n|\to 0</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="4" depends="3" order="9">
          <div>Conversely, suppose <m>|x_n|\to 0</m>. We will prove that <m>x_n\to 0</m>. Let <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="5" depends="4" order="1">
          <div>Because <m>|x_n|\to 0</m> and <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>, by definition there exists <m>N\in\R</m> such that for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>\bigl||x_n|-0\bigr|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="6" depends="5" order="6">
          <div>Let <m>n\in\N</m> with <m>n\gt N</m>. Then <m>|x_n-0|=|x_n|=\bigl||x_n|-0\bigr|\lt\epsilon</m>. By definition, <m>x_n\to 0</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="7" correct="no" order="3">
          <div>Choose <m>N=\dfrac{1}{\epsilon}</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="8" correct="no" order="7">
          <div>Because <m>x_n\to 0</m>, for every <m>n\in\N</m> we have <m>|x_n-0|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="9" correct="no" order="4">
          <div>Since for every <m>n\in\N</m>, <m>|x_n|=x_n</m>, the two statements say the same thing.</div>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <!-- #11: Ross 8.10, a fixed tolerance L - a -->
    <exercise label="parsons-stay-above" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide">
      <title>Terms that stay above <m>a</m></title>
      <statement><div>Suppose <m>x_n\to L</m> and <m>L\gt a</m>. Put the given blocks in a correct order to prove that there exists <m>N\in\R</m> such that for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>x_n\gt a</m>. Some blocks are not needed.</div></statement>
      <blocks>
        <block name="1" order="3">
          <div>We assume that <m>x_n\to L</m> and <m>L\gt a</m>. We will prove that there exists <m>N\in\R</m> such that for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>x_n\gt a</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="2" depends="1" order="7">
          <div>Because <m>x_n\to L</m> and <m>L-a\gt 0</m>, by definition there exists <m>N\in\R</m> such that for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>|x_n-L|\lt L-a</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="3" depends="2" order="1">
          <div>We show that this <m>N</m> works.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="4" depends="3" order="9">
          <div>Let <m>n\in\N</m> with <m>n\gt N</m>. Then <m>|x_n-L|\lt L-a</m>, so <m>-(L-a)\lt x_n-L</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="5" depends="4" order="5">
          <div>Adding <m>L</m> to both sides gives <m>x_n\gt L-(L-a)=a</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="6" depends="5" order="2">
          <div>Thus for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>x_n\gt a</m>, as desired.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="7" correct="no" order="6">
          <div>Let <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="8" correct="no" order="8">
          <div>Choose <m>N=L-a</m>.</div>
        </block>
        <block name="9" correct="no" order="4">
          <div>Because <m>x_n\to L</m> and <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m>, by definition there exists <m>N\in\R</m> such that for all <m>n\gt N</m>, <m>|x_n-L|\lt\epsilon</m>.</div>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
